    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe absolute value of a number is its distance from zero when viewed on a number line.\n\nContent:\nDistances are never negative. Therefore, the absolute value of a number is always positive or zero.\n\nContent:\nLet's consider the number [MATH: -5] and its position on a number line, shown below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/cf920d91f72b1615210264e172d01bc3]\n\nContent:\nThe distance between [MATH: -5] and zero equals [MATH: 5] because [MATH: -5] is five steps away from zero.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/18e8471cb3232c92db80fd4281fcc721]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, the absolute value of [MATH: -5] equals [MATH: 5.] We write this as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: | \\, {-5} \\, | = 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Tutorial: A Fast Way of Finding the Absolute Value\n\nContent:\nThere is a fast way to find the absolute value of a number:\n\nContent:\n- The absolute value of a positive number is equal to itself. For example,\n[MATH: |8| = 8 .]\n- To find the absolute value of a negative number, we drop the negative sign. For instance,\n[MATH: | {-8} | = 8.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Example: Finding the Absolute Value of a Positive Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\mid 4\\!\\mid \\,?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe absolute value of a positive number equals the number:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\mid 4\\!\\mid \\, =4\n}]\n\nThe absolute value of a number gives the distance between the number and zero on a number line.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/ecf82ab57681530dc61be205de984f04]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u22233\u2223=]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 6]\nb. [MATH: 6]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 3]\nd. [MATH: 3]\ne. [MATH: 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: |(16)/(3)|=]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 5]\nb. [MATH: 6]\nc. [MATH: (16)/(3)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (16)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Finding the Absolute Value of a Negative Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind [MATH: | {-2} |.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo find the absolute value of [MATH: -2,] we simply drop the negative sign:\n[MATH: \\mid-2\\!\\mid=2]\n\nThe absolute value of a number gives the distance between the number and zero on a number line.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/dff1a8683a8801088a3ab41a97e525ef]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2223 \u2212 6\u2223=]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 3]\nc. [MATH: 6]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 6]\ne. [MATH: 4]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: |\u2212 (5)/(2)|=]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 3]\nb. [MATH: 2]\nc. [MATH: (5)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (5)/(2)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 2]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Example: Finding a Number Given Its Absolute Value and Position on a Number Line\n\nExample Prompt:\nThe point shown below represents a number on a number line. The absolute value of this number is [MATH: 4.] What is the number?\n\n[IMG: /graphics/5d582147fd8d78c85f93d1c0d92da868]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe number is negative because it is to the left of zero.\n\nThe absolute value of the number is [MATH: 4,] so the distance between that number and zero on a number line is [MATH: 4.]\n\nThe negative number at a distance of [MATH: 4] from zero is [MATH: -4,] so the number is [MATH: -4.]\n\n[IMG: /graphics/eb80c5a808a4166ec5dcdabb8d6a63d0]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 5\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-61631]\n\nPrompt:\nThe point shown above represents a number on a number line. The absolute value of this number is [MATH: 5.] What is the number?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 5]\nb. [MATH: 3]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 3]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 5]\ne. [MATH: 6]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 6\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-61626]\n\nPrompt:\nThe point shown above represents a number on a number line. The absolute value of this number is [MATH: 3.] What is the number?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 2]\nc. [MATH: 4]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 3]\ne. [MATH: 3]"
  }
